  "level": "L1",
  "reply": "## Section 2.1 Motivation — Brief Summary\n\nSection 2.1 explains the motivation behind the proposed **contextual augmentation** method. The authors use an example sentence from the Stanford Sentiment Treebank (SST): *“the actors are fantastic.”* (positive label). They point out that prior synonym-based augmentation methods can only replace the word “actors” with a limited set of synonyms from WordNet (e.g., histrion, player, thespian, role_player). However, for effective data augmentation, the word “actors” could be replaced with many more non-synonym words such as *characters, movies, stories, songs*, or various other nouns, while still preserving the positive sentiment and naturalness of the sentence. Training with a wider variety of patterns would boost model generalization. Therefore, the authors propose using a **bi-directional language model (LM)** to predict substitute words based on context, which can produce words that have **paradigmatic relations** with the original word, even if they are not synonyms. This is the core motivation for moving beyond synonym-based augmentation to a more flexible, context-aware approach.",
